ould start up and it would run  and it would she be launched and  everything would go fine but one time  they decided ahead of time to do a test  on the launch control system and they  got to the point where they started our  camera not knowing that whenever they  stopped their test our camera would keep  running so our camera just kept in ran  all the film out without the rocket  going anywhere and so that was one of  the few times that we didn't get the  launch the launch shot that we wanted  because it kind of fit there today  before we didn't go any quicker how  close can you get  the cameras are remote but where are you  sometimes you're abandoning a camera to  actually tell me because the firebox  shots are static the closest they let  anybody get during a launch is one mile  from a shuttle and that was the advanced  rescue crew they were stationed inside a  little m13 tank that they could drive  him up and help astronauts that they had  to in an emergency but everybody else  was at least three miles away so we  would be back at a press side or a  remote camera site and to shoot it with  the IMAX camera that that was man  occasionally we would put a camera on  one of NASA's trackers what they would  do is there would be a guy in launch  control and he would anticipate the  shuttle launching and start to tilt up  so that he could be in time with the  shuttle and he would just follow it up  with this tracker and it's like you have  a man camera right there on the launch  pad but it was run remotely as well so  there's all sorts of tricks we used to  get you up close and personal with these  launches for the movies that we've been  doing  so leo what was like I mean you're in  you're in you know the the headquarters  of NASA you're at consoles you're  listening to astronauts that must have  been a pretty cool thing to be doing yes  it was a very surreal experience since  that's that it's a different world  inside that all this communication going  on and you're having to listen on  various loops  we're also retrieving data we were  monitoring the current buses that were  providing power to our cameras so we had  sort of an indication if a camera had  had done a shot or not because it had a  specific signature that you could see on  the on the on the powered rock and there  were there are other clues that would  help too and and the production people  very much interested in having this  information so they could sort of piece  together these pieces to help them  understand the shot better that was  being made so you know as we move  forward you've got to look to the future  what do you guys you know see is the  future is IMAX IMAX in space you know we  did a beautiful planet  I think our days are probably gone  shooting shooting film and space but  Leah what would you like to see us do  next you know all these space films are  spectacular and when NASA does something  unique it's it's always another  opportunity in which we could film if we  have if if we if we're in the right  position to be able to do that make that  arrangement with them to be able to do  that hopefully I to the moon and let's  put in I would love an IMAX camera on  Mars  someday I hope I live to see that day  what do you think's in our future James  uh well I I would really like to see  some IMAX shot on the moon I think  physically on the moon shooting back at  Earth I got a shot that I want to do you  know how we  the corona of the Sun during solar  eclipse from Earth well we're gonna see  the corona of the Sun blocked out from  the earth blocking it out rather than  the moon blocking of course the Earth's  wouldn't have an atmosphere and so  you're gonna have this these beautiful  sunset colors all the way around the  earth during this Eclipse as well as the  solar corona going out from it I think  it's probably going to be one of the  most stunning sight that a human will  ever see in the very near future apart  from that I think going to Mars is going  to be a great thing to do of course all  these will have to be digital capture  things we can't fly still about the Kurd  back this won't work so looking forward  to getting some really super high res  digital imaging up to now on the moon to  the to the lunar gateway to the Mars  gateway and on to Mars as well I don't  think there's really anything more more  amazing than watching these movies in  the huge big screens IMAX especially  Hubble the 3d sequence IMAX really makes  you understand we're just your little  grains of we're so insignificant things  but makes you really humble and the Dome  systems our Dome systems really are  amazing to really get you immersed you  know Patricia and I in a simply content  last year and we had a feed from the  space station and nobody knew that IMAX  is in the audience what images from the  space program inspired you when you were  younger  so it is like the dream is alive Hill  Columbia I grew up in a small town we  did have an IMAX theater theater  anything so I actually saw my first IMAX  film when I went to Space Camp and it  completely blew my mind and I was  enthralled with it just the fact that  you know with the wraparound screen you  felt completely immersed and every year  that I went back to Space Camp I saw the  same movies but I never got tired of  them there's three or four or five  astronauts as children they saw our  movies and really propelled them into  thinking about being astronauts and  hopefully we continue to do that as we  move forward with new digital capture  techniques new new sound techniques  we've got 12 channels sound now in our  IMAX theatres and I certainly can't wait  along with all my colleagues to get back  to the movies and I thank you both you  guys for being instrumental in getting  getting Hubble made and I think our  theatres and all our partners around the  world who shown it to children and  educated them and we can't wait to  continue so thanks very much guys thank  you thank yo